<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yevgenia Bosch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yevhenia Bosh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yevgenia Bosch was a Bolshevik revolutionary who served as People's Secretary of Internal Affairs in the Soviet government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> formed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in December 1917, and is often described as the first woman to head a modern government. She took part in the Civil War as a political commissar and later joined the Left Opposition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yevgenia-bosch.jpg" alt="Yevgenia Bosch" width="252" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown-anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugenia_Bosz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yevgenia Bosch, born Meisch, came from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakiv</a> in the Kherson Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Her father, Gotlieb Meisch, was a German colonist, mechanic and landowner, and her mother, Maria Krusser, a Bessarabian noblewoman. She attended the Voznesensk women's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> and as a young woman married Peter Bosch, the owner of a small wagon shop, with whom she had two daughters. She joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1901 and its Bolshevik faction in 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a police search of her house in 1906 she left her husband for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, where she worked in the underground with her sister Elena Rozmirovich and later with her partner Georgy Pyatakov. The two led the Kyiv committee until their arrest and exile to the Irkutsk Governorate in 1912, from which they escaped by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. There they formed the Baugy group with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin">Nikolai Bukharin</a> and others, which opposed Lenin on the national question.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> she returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, came round to Lenin's position and headed party committees of the Southwestern region. She helped launch the First All-Ukrainian Congress of Soviets at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in December 1917, which proclaimed a Soviet republic in federation with Soviet Russia and elected her to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat">People's Secretariat</a>. In March 1918 she resigned in protest at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> forces of Vladimir Antonov-Ovseyenko. She later chaired the party committee of the Penza Governorate, served on the Caspian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> front and was a political commissar in the war against General Denikin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Suffering from tuberculosis and heart disease, Bosch was incapacitated from 1922. She joined the Left Opposition in 1923 and signed the Declaration of 46. Despairing after Trotsky was forced out as leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and in pain from her illnesses, she shot herself in January 1925. The government played down her standing: she had only a local funeral and a short obituary in Pravda. Her memoirs, A Year of Struggle, appeared the same year, and a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> bore her name from 1925 until its destruction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yevgenia Bosch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government, called the People's Secretariat, included <b>Yevhenia Bosh</b>, Mykola Skrypnyk and Volodymyr Zatonsky.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic-of-soviets">Ukrainian People's Republic of Soviets</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Yevgenia Bosch"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 5342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
